# Resource monitoring

Pau samples each process tree that it starts, including agent sessions, terminals,
external editors, and supported stdio MCP servers. Open a workspace's
**Processes** view to compare the trees. Open a session inspector for a compact
summary of that session.

The views keep model context, token counts, and cost separate from machine
resources.

## Read the measurements

Each active tree reports:

- CPU use across the root process and its descendants
- Current and peak resident memory
- Current and peak process count
- Elapsed time and accumulated CPU time
- Disk bytes read and written when the operating system reports reliable totals

Expand a process row to inspect its root process and descendants. Pau identifies
a process by its PID and start time, so a reused PID does not inherit an earlier
process's history.

Disk I/O can show **Unavailable**. This means Pau cannot obtain a reliable
per-process total on the current platform. It does not mean that the process
performed no I/O.

## History and completed work

Pau samples active trees every two seconds and keeps about 30 minutes of raw
samples in memory. Raw samples are not written to the event replay log or sent
to a telemetry service.

When a process ends, Pau stores a small summary with elapsed time, accumulated
CPU time, peak memory, peak process count, and disk totals when available. The
raw history is discarded.

## Respond to sustained pressure

A warning appears only after CPU or memory pressure continues across several
samples. Pau keeps the work running. Use **Interrupt work** to stop the current
agent turn while preserving the session, or **Stop session** to end the managed
process. These actions appear only when the agent adapter supports them.

Pau does not terminate, throttle, or restart work automatically in response to a
resource warning.
